          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I was told my thyroid is underactive.",
          "answer": "An underactive thyroid is called **hypothyroidism**. It means the thyroid gland does not make enough thyroid hormone. The thyroid hormones set how fast most of the body's tissues work. The source is the *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)* summary in the general clinical reference in this project.\n\nThe same source explains that the pituitary gland, a small gland in the brain, releases a signal hormone (thyroid-stimulating hormone, or TSH) to prompt the thyroid. When the thyroid is underactive, TSH often rises before the thyroid hormone level falls out of range. That is why TSH is usually the first blood test.\n\nMy search did not return information on symptoms, causes, or treatment, so I can't tell you more from this source. Your clinician is the best person to explain what your result means for you. You could ask:\n\n- What were my test results, and what do the numbers mean?\n- Do I need any follow-up tests?\n- What are the next steps, and what symptoms should prompt me to contact you sooner?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
